  "text": "proprium.dominica-xix-post-pentecosten-evangelium",
  "about": "The evangelium of the formulary “19th Sunday after Pentecost” in the 1962 Roman Missal.",
  "segments": {
    "s01": { "translation": "At that time, Jesus was speaking to the chief priests and the Pharisees in parables, saying: “The kingdom of heaven has become like a king who gave his son a wedding feast. And he sent his servants to summon those who had been invited to the wedding, and they were unwilling to come. Again he sent other servants, saying: ‘Tell those who have been invited: “Behold, I have prepared my dinner; my bulls and fatlings have been slaughtered, and all things are ready; come to the wedding.”’ But they disregarded it and went away, one to his estate and another to his trade; and the rest seized his servants, heaped outrages upon them, and killed them. When the king heard of it, he was angry; and sending his armies, he destroyed those murderers and set their city on fire. Then he said to his servants: ‘The wedding is ready indeed, but those who had been invited were not worthy. Go, therefore, to the highways, and whomever you find, call them to the wedding.’ And his servants went out into the roads and gathered together all whom they found, both bad and good; and the wedding was filled with guests at table. Now the king came in to see the guests at table, and he saw there a man who was not clothed in a wedding garment. And he said to him: ‘Friend, how did you come in here without a wedding garment?’ But he was speechless. Then the king said to the attendants: ‘Bind him hand and foot and cast him into the outer darkness: there will be weeping and gnashing of teeth.’ For many are called, but few are chosen.”" }
